EXPERIMENT-7: Web Service using JAX-WS (SOAP)
=============================================

REQUIREMENT:
- Java JDK 8 must be installed (JAX-WS was REMOVED in Java 11+)
- Check your version: java -version
- If version is 11 or above, this will NOT work without extra libraries.
  Use Java 8: https://www.oracle.com/java/technologies/javase/javase8-archive-downloads.html
- You need 2 separate terminal windows open in this folder

Steps to Run:

1. Compile all files (this creates a 'calculator' subfolder automatically):
   javac -d . CalculatorInterface.java CalculatorService.java CalculatorPublisher.java CalculatorClient.java

   ERROR: 'javac' is not recognized?
   >> Install JDK 8 and add to PATH.

   ERROR: package javax.jws does not exist?
   >> You are using Java 11 or later. JAX-WS is not included.
   >> Switch to Java 8, or add the JAX-WS dependency manually.

   ERROR: cannot find symbol @WebService?
   >> Same as above — switch to Java 8.

2. Start the Web Service Publisher (Terminal 1 — keep it open):
   java calculator.CalculatorPublisher

   >> Wait until you see: "Web Service running at:"
   >> and: "http://localhost:8080/calculator?wsdl"
   >> Do NOT close this window.

   ERROR: Address already in use (port 8080)?
   >> Another program is using port 8080.
   >> Change 8080 to 9090 in CalculatorPublisher.java and CalculatorClient.java, then recompile.

3. Run the Client (Terminal 2 — after Publisher is running):
   java calculator.CalculatorClient

   >> Should print: Result = 30

   ERROR: Connection refused / WebServiceException?
   >> Publisher is not running. Make sure Step 2 is done and still open.

   ERROR: Failed to access the WSDL?
   >> Open a browser and go to http://localhost:8080/calculator?wsdl
   >> If it doesn't open, the Publisher didn't start properly.

NOTE: Start Publisher FIRST, then run the Client.
NOTE: You can verify the service is running by visiting http://localhost:8080/calculator?wsdl in a browser.